00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.310 align:start position:0%
 
it's been over a year since apple's m1  chip was revealed and to this day the m1  macbook air is a killer value and a  great choice for a lot of people but now  apple's working on a brand new m2 chip  that's gonna be at the heart of apple's  upcoming lower end max like the  redesigned macbook air and probably even  the new ipad pro so the big question  that we've got to figure out is this how  much of a performance boost should we  expect with the new m2 chip both in  terms of the cpu and gpu performance so  without further ado let's jump right  into it now the most important detail in  terms of the m2 chip that we have to  understand is this report from mark  german from back in may he said that the  macbook air with the m2 chip is coming  with a 9 or 10 core gpu so what he  essentially means by that is that  instead of the current m1 chips 7 or 8  gpu cores depending on which model you  get  we're going to be getting either 9 or 10  gpu cores with the m2 chip but if we dig  deeper into his actual report on  bloomberg.com he says that the new chip  which is going to be codenamed statin is  going to include the same number of  computing cores as the m1 but run faster  so that means we're getting the same  four high performance cores and the same  for efficiency cores but they will be  faster now this is way different  compared to the new m1 pro and m1 max  chips that we have in the new macbook  pros which have eight high performance  cores and only two efficiency cores so  because of that the m2 chip will stay  much slower than the m1 pro as i'm going  to show you very soon and before we get  into calculating the performance for  both the cpu and the gpu side we have to  answer a couple of different big  questions like which processing node  will the m2 be built on because  originally we thought it was going to be  built on the three nanometer process  from tsmc but it does look like that was  delayed and then a little bit later we  found out that the new a16 bionic chip  in the upcoming iphone 14 is gonna be  based on tsmc's four nanometer process  but then this article was updated to say  that tsmc actually refers to it as n4p  which is not four nanometer at all it's  actually the third major enhancement of  the five nanometer family so with that  said if the m2 chip is in fact gonna be  built on the third major enhancement of  five nanometer here is what we should  expect first of all it says that n4p  will deliver an 11  performance boost over the original n5  technology now if you don't already know  the m1 chip is actually built on the  original so it should have an 11 boost  over the m1 even if nothing else changes  and we have basically the same design we  should get at least 11 so with that said  let's go ahead and get into the  calculations so if we start with the  single core geekbench score of the m1  which is a thousand seven hundred and  fourteen points and then add eleven  percent to that we should be getting  1902  single core points which is actually  very impressive and then if we apply  that 11 jump to the multi-core  performance we should be getting at  least 8  278 points which is actually faster than  the 8 core mac pro and going even  further tsmc says that compared to n5  n4p will also deliver a 22 improvement  in power efficiency as well so that  means we could get up to 22 better  battery life if the performance stays  the same which is very interesting  however i don't know if you guys saw  those renders of the m2 macbook air  which you probably have the new design  is incredibly thin so maybe apple is  going to make the battery size smaller  and keep the battery life the same just  to give us a really nice thin design but  who knows and now with that said we do  have the major dilemma of whether apple  is going to be actually going to rnv9  with this m2 chip or not because if  apple sticks to the same a15 bionic  cores and gpu cores those actually did  not have rnv9 support they were still on  rv8 so if apple uses a15 cores then the  new m2 chip is not gonna have rnv9 so  that basically gives us a bunch of  conflicting information because we can't  have a15 cores and arm v9 at the same  time if we are truly getting rv9 on the  m2 chip than the m2 actually has to be  based on next year's a16 chip for the  iphone 14. so in my opinion there are  actually two possible scenarios for the  m2 chip scenario one is that apple  completely holds off an rv9 and the new  features like better security sve 2  support and more and they just base the  m2 off of the a15 chip which can make  sense and then scenario 2 is that of  course the m2 chip is based on the next  generation a16 bionic chip which comes  with rnv9 support so with that said let  me go through both of those scenarios in  the first case with scenario 1 the  process node will no longer be 3rd gen 5  nanometer and if we go back and look at  the difference between the single core  performance of apple's a14 chip and the  a15 which basically shows the difference  between first and second gen 5 nanometer  we're actually getting a larger nine  percent performance boost instead of  tsmc's quoted six percent boost so  that's probably coming from extra  additions like the doubling of system  cache and other design changes including  clock speed improvements and now since  we know that the m1 chip is built on  first gen 5 nanometer we can apply that  same 9 boost going up to the second gen  5 nanometer with the m2 chip which gives  us a single core score of around  1876 points which is actually quite a  good improvement even without needing  the new rnv9 changes but then things get  a little bit weird when we take a look  at the multi-core performance jump from  the a14 to the a15 where we actually get  a huge 18 jump in terms of iphone  performance and this doesn't really make  sense because the single core  performance only went up by nine percent  so how in the world are we getting an 18  performance jump well as it turns out  the a15 chip cpu is made out of two high  performance cores and four efficiency  cores and apparently the efficiency  cores themselves got a huge boost in  performance this time around and we can  actually calculate how much performance  they'll collectively add by multiplying  the single core score by two and then  subtracting that from the total  multi-core score to reveal the total  score that's coming from the efficiency  cores alone and based on that the  efficiency cores and the a15 chip are a  massive 55  faster than the set that's in the a14  channel so now that we know all of that  about the a14 to a15 chip jump we can  actually use it to predict the  performance of the m2 chip if we take  that single core score of 1876 that we  predicted for the m2 performance cores  and multiply it by four to give us four  high performance scores we get  7504 points from the performance cores  alone and then if we add in the  1234 points from the new a15 efficiency  cores alone we're left with a total  multi-core score of 8738  for the m2 champ but now with that said  we also have the possibility of the  second scenario with the m2 chip  actually being based on the new a16 chip  in the upcoming iphone 14 instead of the  a15 chip which means that it's almost  certainly going to be built on the new  rv9 architecture and if you think that's  completely unrealistic keep in mind that  in 2020 the m1 ship was revealed only 18  days after we got our hands on the  iphone 12 which packs the same a14 ship  that the m1 is based on so next year  apple could easily release the iphone 14  with the a16 chip in september and then  a month or two later they can reveal the  new m2 chip which could be based on the  a16 instead of the old a15 so that's  totally reasonable and with that said we  can bring out the numbers from the  beginning of this video when we added in  an 11 performance boost going from first  to third gen five nanometer process node  and then add in some more performance  improvements thanks to arm v9 and  probably extra cash like we saw in the  a15 and i'm personally gonna estimate a  conservative 20 performance boost over  the m1 with everything combined together  giving us a massive single core score of  2057  which would make it the fastest  performance core in the world but then  switching over to multi-core performance  i'd expect a conservative 25 performance  boost over the m1 ship giving us a  multi-car score of 9322  which actually outperforms the current  fully specced out 27 inch intel imac so  as you can see the new m2 chip is still  going to be a beast in terms of  performance but now let's finally get  into estimating the graphics performance  with both of these scenarios and the  first thing we've got to do is figure  out how much faster the a15 gpu cars are  compared to the a14 the best score we  got with the a14 chip was within the  larger ipad air 4 chassis scoring 12  488 points compared to 14 437  with the iphone 13 pro and the new a15  chip making the iphone 15.6  faster but the weird thing is that it  actually has a 5 core gpu instead of 4  with the a14  so it technically should have scored at  least 25 higher but i think that the  smaller iphone chassis is throttling  down the performance due to heat and one  thing we know for sure is that the gpu  cores and the current eight core gpu m1  macbook air are also throttled because  it scores up to around 22 000 metal  points but if we take the score from the  four core a14 ipad air and then double  it to match the a core gpu in the  macbook air it should technically be  able to get around 25 000 metal points  but it doesn't so there are some  diminishing returns so because of all  this iphone throttling confusion i think  the safest bet would be to take the 14  437 score from the five core a15 gpu and  then double it to create a very  conservative theoretical score for a 10  core m2 chip giving us 28  874 metal points which is a solid  improvement and then for scenario two if  the m2 chip is actually based on the a16  chip then we can add in another 10  conservative boost on top of that due to  the 3rd gen 5 nanometer process node and  other design improvements giving us a  total score of 31  762. so with all that said these  calculations in terms of the performance  should give you some perspective in  terms of whether you should wait for the  m2 max or just buy one of the m1 or m1  pro max right now so hopefully you  enjoyed this video and if you did go  ahead and click the circle above to  subscribe for more videos like this one  and definitely check out one of those  two right over there thanks for watching  and we'll see you in the next video  [Music]

